Micro Magic's Schematic User Environment (MMI_SUE4.4) or SUE for short.


Running SUE:
============

Please read the SUE manual (either sue_manual.txt for a text file or
sue_manual.html) for an introduction/user's guide to SUE.  Note that
this file is also displayed when you hit "SUE manual" in the "Help"
menu in SUE.

Assuming SUE is correctly installed (see below if not), run SUE by typing:

	sue

or if you are really clever,

	sue <-private> <file1> <file2> ... <-MODE verilog|spice> \
		<-LIB library> <-AUTOLIB library> <-new schematic_name> \
		<-CMD sue_command> <-SET variable=value> <-ICONIFY bogus>

where <file1>, <file2> are optional SUE schematics to load after any
libraries are added.  You can see these options with:

	sue -help

The <-private> switch must immediately follow the "sue" command and
will bring up SUE with a private colormap.

Note that multiple libraries may be included as:

	-LIB /foo/lib1 -LIB /foo/lib2     or
	-AUTOLIB /foo/lib1 -AUTOLIB /foo/lib2     or

"LIB" libraries will be available but will not be visible in the icons
listboxes while "AUTOLIB" libraries will (AUTOLIB for AUTOmatically
loaded LIBraries).  Note libraries can also be automatically loaded by
the ./.suerc file (see User Customization below).

SUE commands such as "api_netlist" or "exit" can also be entered at
startup.  Thus if you had a schematic that you just wanted to netlist
in "batch mode", you could use the line:

	sue foo.sue -CMD api_netlist -CMD exit -ICONIFY 1

This would bring up SUE iconified, netlist the cell foo.sue, and then
exit.  This mode is not really batch mode since SUE requires the GUI
to run, but with the iconify option it behaves pretty much like batch mode.

SUE is really designed to be used most effectively with hotkeys.  Yes,
you can do just about everything with the menus -- but it is painful.
If you really like to see menus or when learning the hotkeys, it is
useful to select the "------------------" option in the menu (the top
of each menu).  This will "tear off" the menu and make it a separate
toplevel window.  The hotkeys for each menu item are displayed next to
the item in the menu.  If there is no letter, then no hotkey.  Note
that some functions, such as double-clicking on an icon to edit its
properties, are in no menus.  These "non-menu" hotkeys are enumerated
in the beginning of the SUE manual.


Installing SUE:
===============

The file ${MMI_TOOLS}/sue/.suerc contains all the user-modifiable
options in SUE, including pointers to tools, hotkey definitions,
colors, etc.  Note that this is a TCL file and requires correct TCL
syntax.  When installing SUE, read thru this file to determine what
needs to be modified on your system and copy only those lines into the
site .suerc file located in ${MMI_TOOLS}/../mmi_local/sue/.suerc (or
overridden by the MMI_LOCAL unix environment varaible).  See
the User Customization section of the SUE manual for a more complete
description of the workings of the .suerc files.

SUE should run fine on any system.  However, to interact with tools
such as spice, verilog, irsim, and waveform viewers, the system .suerc
file should be updated to point to the correct executables.

Also included with the SUE distribution are scripts for executing
hspice, powermill, and adm (a fast spice program from Avanti now called
Star-Sim or some such -- and can you believe they actually think they
can rename hspice to be Star-Hspice).  They are called "spice",
"powermill_go", and "adm_go".  You need to configure these scripts for
you own environment.  If you are only running hspice, then you only
need to configure the "spice" script.  

The "spice" script allows you to run spice on either your local
machine, if you have a floating license, or a remote machine if you
have a node-locked license or are running on Linux.

On completion, all three scripts will signal SUE to pop up or
reset the waveform viewers.  Note that NST currently doesn't
read the powermill output format.

When spice netlisting, SUE will include a default spice header if it
can't find <schematic_name>.h.  You should change this default spice
header to load the appropriate spice models and other parameters.

SUE comes with some example libraries (i.e. directories of icons)
which are found in <SUE_DIR>/schematics.  The devices directory
contains generic devices like name nets, globals, I/O's, title bars,
etc.  They should be universal for all designs.  Also, you should only
modify these device icons with extreme caution because SUE assumes
certain things about some of them.  Also included are spice libraries
for doing transistor level circuit design, standard cell icons, etc.
You can merge these libraries, modify them, or change them however you
want.  These icon libraries are not complete nor perfect (especially
the verilog one which is nearly useless).  The SUE MODE determines
which library to use in addition to specifying how to netlist.  Note
that the difference between the spice and mspice (default) mode is
that mspice adds a "u" for micron onto mosfet widths given to it when
netlisting.  Some spice models automatically scale their parameters in
which case you would use spice instead of mspice.

For systems with two heads, you can set the following UNIX environment
variable

PROBE_DISPLAY (environment):
	probe display.  Default to current.  For two display systems,
	set this to the display other than where SUE is.  Can be set
	to be "other" for the other display.

The script that starts sue (${MMI_TOOLS}/sue/sue) attempts to add
fonts to the X font path using the "xset +fp" function.  We have found
that this doesn't work on some systems (please tell us if you know how
to fix this).  If this doesn't work on your system, you will have to
comment out these lines in the sue script and change the font_mode to
"standard" or, if you have a font server, "font_server" in the system
.suerc.  If you selected "standard", you might also have to change the
smallest font in the variable FONT(tiny) from nil2 to something that
your system has like 5x8 if you don't have nil2.

MAX cross-probing.  If you intend for SUE to interact with MAX, they
do so using the sim format.  The sim format doesn't understand any
spice parameters that you might have in your transistor libraries,
such as ln_min and lp_min.  All spice parameters must be defined in
the .suerc file using the SIM_PARAMS array.  For example, if you are
using a 0.25u technology, you might add the following:

set SIM_PARAMS(ln_min) 0.24u
set SIM_PARAMS(lp_min) 0.24u


Limitations:
============

MULTIPLE SCHEMATIC WINDOWS:

TK (SUE's underlying GUI) does not permit windows to be reparented.
Consequently, to move a schematic from one window to another would
require cloning the schematic.  Cloning is not supported and copying
messes up certain things like undo, not to mention being slow.
TKSteal reportedly allows reparenting ...


RENAME:

You cannot rename schematics/icons in SUE.  You must instead copy them
to a new name and then delete (if desired) the originals.  Note that
if a "renamed" icon is instantiated into other schematics, it will
not automatically be renamed when you do this copying action.  Why this
lossage in SUE?  SUE uses the schematic/icon name in the window name
and the window name cannot be changed.
